        <div id="engines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Engines: Displacement, Valves and Starting
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most walk-behind gas mowers use single-cylinder overhead-valve engines in the range of roughly 140 to 190 cc. Larger displacement gives more torque, which helps in thick grass and on self-propelled models that drive the wheels as well as the blade. For a 21-inch push mower, the smaller end of that range is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Displacement is not the whole story. Engine brand and design affect reliability, ease of starting and parts availability. Briggs and Stratton, Kohler, and the in-house engines from major brands are all common, and most local small-engine shops can service them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Easy-start features vary. Many modern mowers start with a single pull thanks to automatic chokes, and some offer electric start. A mower that starts reliably is largely a function of fresh fuel and clean filters, so do not pay a large premium for start features alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check whether the engine uses a dipstick and drain plug or requires tipping the mower to change oil. Some newer engines are designed to stand vertically for storage without leaking oil into the air filter; if storage space is tight, that is worth looking for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Engine warranties are often separate from the mower warranty and handled by the engine maker's service network. Check who services each component near you before buying, especially if you live far from a big-box store or dealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Deck Width and Deck Material
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 21-inch deck is the standard for gas walk-behinds because it balances coverage against weight and maneuverability. A 22-inch deck covers slightly more ground, and 30-inch walk-behinds exist for large yards, but they are much heavier and pricier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck material affects lifespan. Stamped steel decks are common and affordable but will rust over time, especially at the seams, if wet clippings are left inside. Cast aluminum decks do not rust and are found on premium models; they often outlast the engine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck design affects cut and mulch quality. Decks designed for mulching keep clippings suspended long enough to be cut several times. Washout ports let you connect a garden hose to clean under the deck without tipping the mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Height adjustment varies from individual wheel levers to single-point systems that raise the whole deck at once. Single-point adjustment is convenient and encourages you to raise the deck in hot weather, which helps the lawn tolerate heat and drought.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wheel size and wheel bearings matter too. Larger rear wheels roll more easily over uneven ground and reduce scalping. Ball-bearing wheels last longer than plain bushings, which wear and wobble after a few seasons on budget mowers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Fuel and Reliable Starting
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most starting problems with gas mowers come from old fuel. Gasoline begins to degrade within weeks, and ethanol-blended fuel can absorb moisture and leave varnish in the carburetor. A mower that sat with fuel over winter is the classic case of a mower that will not start in spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy fuel in small amounts you will use within about a month, store it in an approved container in a cool place, and add a fuel stabilizer if it will sit longer. Many manufacturers recommend fuel with no more than 10 percent ethanol; never use E15 or higher in a mower unless the manual explicitly allows it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ethanol-free fuel, where available, reduces carburetor problems, and pre-mixed canned fuels designed for small engines are another option for owners who mow infrequently. They cost more per gallon but can prevent expensive carburetor cleanings.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a mower will not start, check the simple things first: fresh fuel, a clean air filter, a good spark plug and a primer bulb that works. Carburetor cleaning or replacement is the next step if old fuel was the cause.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clippings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Bag, Mulch or Side Discharge
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most gas walk-behinds sold today are 3-in-1 mowers: they can collect clippings in a rear bag, mulch them back into the lawn, or throw them out a side chute. Knowing when to use each mode improves both the lawn and the mower's performance, and switching takes less than a minute on most models.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mulching is the best default for a lawn mowed weekly. Fine clippings break down within days and return nitrogen to the soil, which reduces how much fertilizer the lawn needs. It works best when you remove no more than about a third of the grass height in one pass and the grass is dry. Wet or long grass turns mulching into clumping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bagging is useful in spring when growth is fast, in fall when leaves land on the lawn, and any time you want a very tidy finish. A full bag makes a front-drive self-propelled mower lose traction, and emptying it often adds time, so on large lawns many owners bag only when necessary.
          </p>

          <p>
            Side discharge is for overgrown grass. If the lawn has gotten away from you, raise the deck to its highest setting, fit the side chute and make a first pass. Lower the deck a few days later for a second pass with the mulch plug installed. This approach is easier on the engine and the lawn than forcing a mulch cut through tall grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas mowers handle all three modes well because they hold blade speed under load. That is one of the clearest advantages over battery mowers, which often need to slow down or switch to a higher-power mode in dense growth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oil" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Oil Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small engines need clean oil to last. Most manufacturers recommend a first oil change once a new engine is broken in, often within its first five hours, then at least once a season or every 50 hours, whichever comes first. Check the manual for the exact interval and oil type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the oil level before each mow. Running low on oil is one of the quickest ways to damage a small engine. Most walk-behind mowers hold only a small amount, so even a modest leak or consumption can matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Changing oil is easiest when the engine is warm. If your mower has a drain plug, use it; otherwise, many owners tip the mower with the spark plug and air filter facing up to drain through the fill tube. An inexpensive oil extractor pump can make the job cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dispose of used oil properly. Many auto parts stores and municipal recycling centers accept used motor oil for free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the oil weight the manual specifies. Many small engines call for SAE 30 in warm weather or a multi-grade such as 10W-30 for a wider temperature range. Synthetic oils are acceptable in most engines once they are broken in, but check the manual rather than guessing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plug-filter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Spark Plug and Air Filter
          </h2>

          <p>
            A worn or fouled spark plug causes hard starting and rough running. Replace it every season or as the manual recommends; it is an inexpensive part that takes a few minutes to change. Use the plug specified in the manual and check the gap if instructed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The air filter keeps dust and debris out of the engine. A clogged filter starves the engine of air, reducing power and increasing fuel use. Check it regularly, especially in dry, dusty conditions, and replace paper filters when dirty. Foam filters can often be cleaned and re-oiled according to the manual.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a spare plug and filter on hand so you can replace them before the season starts. Many owners do both at the same time as the first oil change of the year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before working on the engine or blade, disconnect the spark plug wire to prevent accidental starting. This is a simple step that prevents serious injuries.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some mowers use a pre-filter or a foam wrap around a paper element. Clean or replace both parts, and make sure the filter cover seals properly when you reinstall it. A gap lets unfiltered air and grit into the engine, which wears the cylinder much faster than normal use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Blade Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sharp blade cuts grass cleanly; a dull blade tears it, leaving ragged tips that turn brown and invite disease. Sharpen the blade at least once a season, more often if you mow a large lawn, sandy soil or hit debris.
          </p>

          <p>
            To remove the blade, disconnect the spark plug wire, tip the mower as the manual directs, and secure the blade with a block of wood while loosening the bolt. Mark the blade's orientation so it goes back the right way up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sharpen with a file or bench grinder, keeping the original angle, then check balance with a simple blade balancer or a nail in the wall. An unbalanced blade causes vibration that can damage the engine. Replace blades that are bent, cracked or worn thin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keeping a spare blade lets you swap quickly and sharpen the dull one later. Clean the deck underside at the same time to remove packed grass that affects airflow and cut quality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Winter Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Proper storage is the most effective way to avoid spring starting problems. At the end of the season, either run the engine until the fuel tank is empty or fill the tank with stabilized fresh fuel and run the engine for a few minutes so treated fuel reaches the carburetor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Change the oil before storage so the engine sits with clean oil. Remove the spark plug and put a small amount of oil in the cylinder if the manual recommends it, then pull the cord slowly to distribute it before reinstalling the plug.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the deck underside, sharpen or replace the blade, and store the mower in a dry place. If you have a battery for electric start, remove it and store it indoors or keep it on a maintainer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not store a mower with fuel in the tank near a furnace, water heater or other source of ignition. Gasoline vapors can travel and ignite.
          </p>

          <p>
            In spring, check the oil level, install a fresh spark plug if you did not at the end of the season, and add fresh fuel. Inspect the blade bolt, the handle fasteners and the drive cable before the first mow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. When a Gas Mower Will Not Start
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work through no-start problems in order of likelihood and cost. Start with fuel: is there fresh fuel in the tank, and has it been there less than a month or so? If the fuel smells sour or looks darker than fresh gasoline, drain it and refill with fresh fuel before trying anything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check the spark plug. Remove it and look at the tip. A wet, black or heavily fouled plug should be replaced. Make sure the plug wire is firmly attached. A new plug costs little and solves a surprising number of hard-start problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at the air filter. A filter clogged with dust or soaked in oil can prevent starting. Replace a paper filter or clean a foam filter according to the manual. If the mower has a primer bulb, press it the recommended number of times and check that it is not cracked.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the mower still will not start, the carburetor is the likely culprit, especially after a winter with old fuel in the system. Carburetor cleaning is a common job at small-engine shops, and on many budget mowers an aftermarket replacement carburetor is inexpensive. Check that the blade brake engages and releases properly as well, since a faulty brake cable can stop the engine from turning over.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prevention beats repair. Treated fuel, a yearly plug and filter, and proper winter storage keep most mowers starting on the first or second pull for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Emissions Rules and Local Restrictions
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas mowers sold in the United States must meet EPA emissions standards, and those sold in California have historically needed CARB certification. California has moved to phase out the sale of most new small off-road gas engines, including lawn mowers, so buyers there will find new gas walk-behinds hard or impossible to buy in stores.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some cities and homeowners associations restrict when gas-powered equipment can be used or limit noise levels. A few municipalities have restricted gas leaf blowers and, in some cases, other gas yard equipment. Check local rules before buying, especially if you live in a dense area.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is a practical consideration even where it is not regulated. Gas mowers are loud enough that hearing protection is recommended, and early-morning mowing can irritate neighbors. If noise is a concern, a battery mower may be the better fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Honda, long a favorite brand for gas walk-behinds, has wound down production of its residential mowers for the US market, so remaining HRX and HRR models are mostly dealer leftover stock. If you find one at a good price, parts support should continue, but it is no longer a mower to plan around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Cost of Ownership
          </h2>

          <p>
            Entry gas push mowers typically cost roughly $250 to $350 at the time of writing. Self-propelled models range from about $350 to $700, and premium self-propelled models with aluminum decks can cost $700 to $1,000 or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Annual upkeep is modest but adds up: oil, a spark plug, an air filter, fuel stabilizer and a blade sharpening or replacement typically cost a few tens of dollars if you do the work yourself. A professional tune-up at a small-engine shop costs more but includes cleaning and inspection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fuel costs depend on lawn size and how often you mow. A walk-behind uses relatively little fuel, but buying small amounts frequently and adding stabilizer adds to the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over the life of a mower, a well-built model with good care often costs less per year than a cheap mower replaced frequently. Choose based on lawn size, terrain and how long you plan to keep the mower.
          </p>

