        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            15. Safe Handling of a Gas Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fill the tank outdoors with the engine off and cool. Spilled fuel on a hot engine can ignite. Wipe up spills and move the mower away from the fueling spot before starting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never run a gas mower in a closed garage or shed. Small engines produce carbon monoxide, which is odorless and can build to dangerous levels in enclosed spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear the lawn of sticks, stones and toys before mowing, and keep children and pets indoors. Wear sturdy shoes, long pants, eye protection and hearing protection. Never reach under the deck while the engine is running.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mow slopes across the face with a walk-behind mower, not up and down, so the mower cannot roll back onto your feet. Avoid mowing wet grass, which can cause slips. After mowing, finish edges with a <a href="/best-weed-eater/">string trimmer</a> rather than tipping the mower to reach them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving fuel in the tank over winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old fuel is the leading cause of mowers that will not start in spring. Either run the tank dry or fill it with stabilized fresh fuel and run the engine so treated fuel reaches the carburetor. Buy fuel in small amounts and use it within about a month.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a push mower for a sloped or large lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pushing a mower up slopes or over a large lawn every week is tiring. If your lawn is over about a quarter acre or has any noticeable slope, a self-propelled mower, preferably with rear-wheel drive on hills, is worth the extra cost. Your back and your weekends will thank you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring oil changes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small engines rely on clean oil. Check the level before each mow and change it at least once a season. Running a mower low on oil or with dirty oil shortens engine life and can cause sudden failure. Keep a quart on the shelf so a top-up is never a reason to skip it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing with a dull blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull blade tears grass, leaving brown tips and inviting disease. Sharpen the blade at least once a season, keep it balanced and replace it if it is bent or worn thin. A spare blade makes this easy. Torn tips turn brown within a day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the mower in an enclosed space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gas engines produce carbon monoxide. Never start or run a mower in a closed garage or shed. Start it outdoors and let it cool before storing it inside. Carbon monoxide builds quickly in a closed space and gives no warning smell. Keep the door open and the engine outside.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

