        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which gas mower should I buy for a typical suburban lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a quarter- to half-acre lawn, the Toro Recycler 22-inch Personal Pace offers an intuitive drive and good mulching at a fair price. If you want a mower that lasts many years, the Toro Super Recycler's aluminum deck is worth the extra cost. For small, flat lawns on a budget, the Craftsman M115 or Troy-Bilt TB110 push mowers are good choices.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is a gas push mower enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pushing works fine on small, flat lawns of up to about a quarter acre. It is lighter, cheaper and simpler than a self-propelled mower. If your lawn is larger, has slopes or you bag clippings often, a self-propelled mower will save effort. Weight and storage space also favor push mowers in small garages and sheds, and fewer moving parts mean fewer repairs over the years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Gas or battery: which suits my lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Battery mowers suit small to mid-size lawns mowed regularly, offering quiet operation and minimal maintenance. Gas mowers suit large lawns, thick or tall grass and irregular mowing, where runtime and sustained power matter. Consider your lawn size, grass conditions and willingness to do upkeep. If you already own a battery tool platform, that tilts the decision toward battery for smaller lawns.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a gas mower's oil be changed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most manufacturers recommend changing oil after the first few hours on a new engine, then at least once a season or about every 50 hours. Check your manual for the exact interval and oil type, and check the level before every mow. Fresh oil is the cheapest insurance a small engine can get, and the job takes about fifteen minutes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the right way to store a gas mower over winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Run the tank dry or fill it with stabilized fresh fuel and run the engine briefly. Change the oil, clean the deck, sharpen the blade and store the mower in a dry place away from ignition sources. Remove and store any starting battery indoors. These steps take under an hour and prevent most spring starting problems.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I budget for a gas mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Entry push mowers typically cost about $250 to $350, self-propelled models roughly $350 to $700, and premium models with aluminum decks $700 or more at the time of writing. Add a few tens of dollars a year for oil, filters, plugs and stabilizer. Spending a little more on a better deck usually pays back in years of extra service.
              </p>
            </div>

